RCP/M - INSTALLATION GUIDE

1. MACOS ARM64: DOWNLOAD, UNPACK, RUN

This package targets Apple Silicon Macs with macOS 11 or later.
The deployment target is 11.0; testing was performed on the build host,
not on every macOS release. Unpack the ZIP into a writable
folder, then open Start-RCPM.command. Keep all files together. No Python,
tmux, compiler, Homebrew or separately installed CP/M tools are required.
The program uses macOS system libraries. About 550 MB is needed after
unpacking. The first start creates unique RBBS passwords in SYSOP.DOC;
keep that file private. No historical users or messages are supplied.

This is an unsigned engineering candidate. macOS download security policy
may require you to approve an identified application through Privacy &
Security. Code signing/notarization and a fresh-Mac download test are still
release qualification work. Do not disable system-wide security settings.

The local terminal is the sysop console. On successful listener creation:

    RCP/M is now running on port 4000.
    Listening address: 127.0.0.1 (direct raw TCP).
    See INSTALLATION.DOC for information on how to connect
    from your system (Windows, Linux, macOS).

Wait for 'RCP/M modem ready.' before connecting. One caller at a time.
This foreground session must stay open while the service runs. There is no
tmux service, background daemon, automatic client launch or auto-reattach.

2. CONNECTING FROM WINDOWS, LINUX OR MACOS

Use your preferred terminal client with a RAW TCP connection to
127.0.0.1 port 4000. Select ANSI/VT100, 80 columns, and carriage-return
line endings. Press Return after connecting if the CP/M prompt does not
appear. The connection is byte-transparent; it is not a Telnet protocol
server. Select RAW rather than Telnet negotiation in the client.

Windows: PuTTY can use Connection type Raw, host 127.0.0.1, port 4000.
SyncTERM can use its Raw connection type on Windows, Linux and macOS.
Linux/macOS: 'nc 127.0.0.1 4000' is useful for a basic connection check;
a proper raw terminal client is preferable for file transfers and editing.
Clients are supplied/installed by the operator, not bundled with RCP/M.
The packaged emulator currently runs on macOS ARM64; these client notes do
not imply native Windows/Linux host binaries have been qualified.

127.0.0.1 is local to the machine running RCP/M. For another computer to
connect, stop RCP/M, edit ADDRESS in RCPM.CONF to the Mac's LAN IPv4
address, restart, and allow that port in the host firewall as appropriate.
Connect clients to that LAN address. Raw TCP provides no encryption.

3. FIRST-START FILES AND ADMINISTRATION

The package supplies A:, I: and P:. P: includes all 310 SIGM and 93 CPMUG
libraries. The first start creates an empty J: mount disk and unique local
RBBS passwords. SYSOP.DOC contains those PRIVATE generated passwords; it is
not the general manual and must not be shared with callers.

Read SYSOP-GUIDE.DOC for routine operation, RBBS administration, uploads,
archive maintenance and backups. DISK-IMAGES.DOC describes the raw image
formats and host editing tools. Keep the emulator stopped during host edits.

4. FIRST SHUTDOWN

BYE logs a caller off; it does not stop the server. To stop locally between
calls, press Ctrl-C, select Return at BYE's menu, wait for the CP/M prompt,
then enter SYSUTLS:BYEBYE. Full operating instructions are in SYSOP-GUIDE.DOC.

5. OPTIONAL VIRTUAL MODEM (TCPSER / IP232)

Direct raw TCP passthrough is the default and requires no virtual modem.
Both compiled BYE5 drivers are in drivers/direct/ and drivers/ip232/.
The launcher installs the driver matching MODE before boot, while stopped.
Never replace the driver on a mounted/running A: disk.

For tcpser, there are TWO ports: its caller port and its IP232 virtual
serial endpoint. cpmsim connects OUT to the IP232 endpoint; callers connect
IN to tcpser's caller port. This is not a TCP proxy in front of the direct
listener. Other virtual modems must implement compatible IP232 control
framing, including DCD and DTR; an arbitrary virtual serial port is not enough.

Install an IP232-capable tcpser build separately if you want this option:
    https://github.com/go4retro/tcpser

Example for the tested go4retro tcpser options (Mac/Linux shell; use the
same flags with a suitable Windows build of that optional program):

    tcpser -v 127.0.0.1:25232 -p 127.0.0.1:4000 -s 2400 -S 2400 -i '&c1&d2' -l 0

Stop RCP/M before switching. Edit RCPM.CONF:

    MODE=ip232
    ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
    PORT=25232

Start tcpser, then Start-RCPM.command. The modem build sends Hayes setup
and uses tcpser carrier detect. Connect callers to tcpser port 4000; do not
connect them to IP232 port 25232. tcpser is optional and is not bundled.

To switch back, stop RCP/M and tcpser, restore these RCPM.CONF values, then
start RCP/M:

    MODE=direct
    ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
    PORT=4000

The native launcher also accepts --mode, --address and --port as temporary
overrides, e.g. './runtime/rcpm --mode direct --port 4003'. These options do
not alter RCPM.CONF. './runtime/rcpm --check' reports configuration only.

6. PROVENANCE AND RELEASE STATUS

MANIFEST.json records the common payload and platform binary checksums.
ARCHIVE.sha256 lists all 403 P: libraries. VOLUMES.json records the curated
J: contents. Sources, patches and build notes are under source/. Original
notices are retained under NOTICES/ and within the original libraries.
Read DISTRIBUTION-STATUS.DOC: this engineering candidate is not yet cleared
for public redistribution. It does not import an operator's live disks.

Microsoft M80/L80 and MicroPro WM (including WM.HLP) are not bundled as development tools.
Running this package does not require them. Builders supply their original
pinned toolchain disk separately; see source/BUILDING.DOC and
DEVELOPMENT-MEDIA.json. The DRI files remain on offline/BUILD.dsk.
